  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nInitial short wave moving into central Kentucky will lift across the\nregion today. A second short wave in western Arkansas will move \nnortheast and cross the area tonight into Saturday.\n\nShowers will continue to spread northwards, making into west central\nOhio during the mid afternoon. The Gulf will be wide open allowing a\nmodest low level jet to transport deep moisture into the region with\nprecipitable water in the vicinity of 1.8 inches. The warm cloud \ndepth will be greater than 3.5 km allowing for more efficient warm \nrain processes. Instability will be rather limited, although some \nembedded thunder may occur with the better chances in southern areas.\n\nLow pressure associated with the second impulse will move into the\narea during the latter part of the night and continue on up to around\nLake Erie on Saturday. An arching warm front extending out from the \nlow will lift across the region tonight. As it passes, widespread\nprecipitation will end with just some spotty light showers in the\nwarm sector for the rest of the night into early Saturday. However,\nadditional showers and storms will develop with the diurnal cycle as\nsome destabilization occurs ahead of surface trough trailing the \nlow.\n\nMost guidance continues with the theme of a swath of heavier \nrainfall along and south/east of I-71. Most of that zone will get at\nleast one inch with both REFS and HEFS indicating that narrow stripes\nof up to 3 inches will be possible. It should be noted that there is\nat least some potential for a secondary heavier axis near the I-75\ncorridor in Ohio if cells can become concentrated where the warm\nfront takes on a more southwest-northeast orientation immediate \nahead of the low. The probability of this occurring is less than 30 \npercent.\n\nMost of northern Kentucky and parts of nearby southeast Indiana and\nsouthern Ohio have moister soils from recent rainfall. Thus it seemed\nprudent to issue a flood watch for the potential of flash flooding\nthis afternoon into the overnight. The northeast extent of the watch\nis a bit of a question. Highland, Ross, and Hocking counties have not\nhad as much recent rain and thus drier soil. But there are some\nflashier areas in these counties, so they have been included. There\nwas also some consideration of extending the watch into central Ohio.\nIf guidance trends continue to indicate that the heaviest rain may\nimpact that part of the area, then an expansion of the watch may be\nneeded. \n\nWith a very moist profile limiting instability, the potential for\nsevere weather looks low. There remains a narrow window late in the\nday, when instability will be maximized, where the warm front will be\nnear the far southern part of the area. Low level wind profiles near\nthe warm front are concerning, so cannot completely rule out rotating\nstorms if any pockets of sufficient instability are manifested. There\nis very little shear on Saturday, so the potential for any stronger,\norganized storms looks very low.",
